  "contracts": [
    "The fixed model exposes compress_ratios. Under async speculative decoding, a valid sampled-count buffer and a nonempty previous mapping, the pinned runner waits for the previous count before CPU seq_len correction and the raw slot producer. The copy helper waits on the sampling stream, copies counts to host on its side stream, and records the event. In the trace a sole side-stream memcpy/record pair follows the target selection before the auxiliary projection, both submitted before context KV. This identifies the applicable mechanism by source, task order and streams. The database does not export host event handles or the individual runtime predicates. Exact per-call event-object identity is unproved.",
    "Count only adjacent graph→graph calls with at least one continuing request, the typed padded route, the unique raw-slot launch, and a following metadata task and worker-main target replay. Consider every worker-main SynchronizeEvent beginning after the 90 context launches and returning before the first raw-slot submission, with return no earlier than the count-ready timestamp minus the selected clock tolerance. No symmetric return-time cutoff. If no candidate or an unsupported transition, charge the full host span. If more than one candidate, take the largest residual benefit. Different main and submit thread IDs remain distinct.",
    "Let H be the full original first-to-last context launch interval. In the fixed count-event model earlier main-thread arrival at a pending unchanged event is absorbed; transferable main-path credit is bounded by min(H,max(0,wait_enter-(record_end-tolerance))). Wait overhead after readiness stays fixed. The conservative queue variant restores all H whenever the separate submitter lacks a full H of CANN inactivity immediately before the candidate return, checked across nested APIs. CANN silence is not proof of Python inactivity or a drained dispatch queue. The measured main-path and the unobserved cross-thread path therefore remain distinct. The wave-local causal mode bounds the exported device-minus-host offset from above using the smallest device start minus linked host API start among current ID, count transfer/event, raw slot, first metadata and next target. No device work can start before its triggering API begins. It assumes a common offset within that short rank-local interval. A second mode grants another 0.25 ms of intra-wave drift. Constant 0.25/1/2/5 ms allowances are reported separately for sensitivity, not as calibrated offsets.",
    "The count producer is upstream of context KV in this schedule. For rejection/selection with direct next-token and valid-count outputs, keep the full sampled IDs, original positional/discard/backup rules and any external readers. Treat the entire measured span from target selection end to the side-stream event as free, including the copy, for an intentionally generous fixed-selection device estimate. Do not charge the pre-existing model or 19 ms wait to this small epilogue. An alternative changing score computation or submission of unrelated work needs separate pricing.",
    "Joint sensitivities grant both the host residual and named device savings as if completely serial on the same rank; overlap can reduce them. The separate-rank free case gives each wave its largest host cap plus its largest entire device span independently, including all required WKV work and gaps for that latter span. This is an intentionally loose budget under the gate assumptions.",
    "For eight clients use the same inclusive-deadline and 16-rank latest-next-entry model as the previous context estimate. The independent-rank sensitivity always credits the largest live rank per wave. No new workload, no benchmark, host-only and device reductions are not additive. Ordinary request dependency/runtime-wait alignment was not recorded by these frozen C8 databases; no C8 wait frequency is transferred to them. For the separate seed+count device epilogue only, price one prompt at the observed C8 min/max span across shapes, plus actual per-request verification count times the T6 median. This is a favorable transfer sensitivity, not an ordinary-service hard bound."
  ],
